! THE CONNECTIVES' BYTE SIZE -- Reference Manual printed p.75, under
! the heading "Byte Size", which this model ignored until 2026-10-19:
!
!   "The connective operations process data in eight-bit bytes.  In
!   these operations, the byte size specified in the instruction
!   designates the number of bits used to represent each character or
!   symbol in the storage field.  IF THE BYTE SIZE OF THE STORAGE
!   OPERAND IS LESS THAN EIGHT, EACH BYTE IS CONVERTED INTO AN
!   EIGHT-BIT BYTE BEFORE PROCESSING BY THE ADDITION OF HIGH-ORDER
!   ZEROS.  The accumulator operand is assumed to have a byte size of
!   eight.  Any offset may be specified and the field is processed in
!   eight-bit bytes starting with the offset. ...  CONSEQUENTLY, THE
!   RESULT BYTES ALWAYS HAVE A BYTE SIZE OF EIGHT."
!
! and, for CONNECT TO MEMORY, the reverse:
!
!   "...the result byte is truncated before being placed in storage
!   if the byte size of the storage field is less than eight.  Each
!   byte in the storage field is replaced by the number of bits
!   specified by the byte size.  These bits are the low-order bits of
!   each result byte; the remaining high-order bits of each result
!   byte are dropped."
!
! ARRIVED VIA MCP, which is also where the test data comes from.
! Printed p.5 line 12 is "LF(BU,21,3),X ERR AD,1" with the comment
! "-BREAK ADDRESS INTO EIGHT BIT BYTES": the error routine spreads the
! seven octal digits of a 21-bit address into seven character
! positions so that two later M+MG can add them into an IQS string
! without carrying between the digits.  SRC below is 3476 = octal
! 0006624, which is what X ERR AD holds when MCP's bootstrap stops on
! this project's fixture tape -- half-word address 869, i.e. 000662.40.
!
! LF IS THE CONNECTIVE C0011.  Figure 20 on printed p.76 gives
! connective 3 the symbolic function "m", the memory bit, so C0011 is
! a plain load of the field; connective 5 is "a", the accumulator bit,
! which is what CM0101 below needs to push the accumulator back out.
!
! WHAT EACH BLOCK ESTABLISHES
!   T1  the expansion itself: seven 3-bit bytes become seven 8-bit
!       bytes, so 21 bits of storage become 56 bits of accumulator.
!   T2  the offset is applied to the EXPANDED field, not the 21-bit
!       one -- the same bytes eight bits further left.
!   T3  "In CONNECT ... the remainder of the accumulator remains
!       unchanged" (p.76): the field is 56 bits, so bits 56-63 of a
!       pre-loaded all-ones accumulator survive and bits 0-55 do not.
!   T4  CONNECT TO MEMORY's contraction, the round trip: the expanded
!       bytes go back out as 3-bit bytes and reproduce SRC exactly.
!   T5  **BYTE SIZE EIGHT IS UNCHANGED**, which is the claim that
!       every other connective test in this suite depends on.  The
!       same 21 bits with the default byte size land as a flat 21-bit
!       field, as they always did.  Note what this block does and
!       does not reach: 21 at byte size 8 is 8+8+5, so it exercises
!       the SHORT high-order byte.  The multiple-of-eight case --
!       every byte masking to 0xFF, no short tail -- is covered by
!       the thirty-odd connective sections already in the suite
!       (C22-6578 Problem 2.2's CT0110, ax1a_bew, the whole of
!       jb_af1c), not by this deck.
!   T6  **THE OFFSET IS BITS, NOT BYTES.**  Offset 1 shifts the
!       whole expanded field one bit left, so every byte's value
!       doubles.  This block exists because the opposite was built
!       first, on three strands that all pointed the same way -- p.75
!       says "the field is processed in eight-bit bytes starting with
!       the offset", p.57 states the exact analogue for decimal ("a
!       multiple of four by ignoring the two low-order bits of the
!       offset"), and MCP's own LF carries offset 1 while the two
!       M+MG that consume its result carry 8 and 0.  **The full suite
!       killed it: 56 sections, all of jb_af1c and the PAU gates,
!       because the floating-point diagnostic checks every result
!       with "CT0110(BU,60),ROS,4" -- a connective at OFFSET 4.**
!       IBM's own diagnostic outranks the inference.  T6 is here so
!       that nobody rebuilds it.

! SIX INJECTIONS, MEASURED, one per block and each landing on its own
! check alone:
!   bsEff forced to 8 (the behaviour before this work): T1 = 3476,
!     T2 = 0xd9400, T3 = 0xffffffffffe00d94, T4 = 0x60204 -- four
!     failures, and T5 and T6 still pass, which is the proof that
!     byte size eight is genuinely untouched.
!   the offset rounded down to a byte: T6 = 0x6060204, and the other
!     five unmoved.  That injection is the REFUTED hypothesis, kept
!     as an injection precisely because it is the plausible wrong
!     answer; the suite's own jb_af1c sections are what rule it out.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

START   L(BU,64,8), ZERO, 0          ! LOAD clears the whole accumulator
        C0011(BU,21,3), SRC+.43, 0   ! seven 3-bit bytes -> seven 8-bit
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0

        L(BU,64,8), ZERO, 0
        C0011(BU,21,3), SRC+.43, 8   ! same bytes, one byte further left
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0

        L(BU,64,8), ALL1, 0          ! accumulator bits 0-63 all ones
        C0011(BU,21,3), SRC+.43, 0   ! replaces bits 0-55 only
        ST(BU,64,8), T3, 0

        L(BU,64,8), WIDE, 0          ! the expanded form, by hand
        CM0101(BU,21,3), T4+.43, 0   ! result = a, contracted to 3 bits

        L(BU,64,8), ZERO, 0
        C0011(BU,21), SRC+.43, 0     ! byte size defaults to 8
        ST(BU,64,8), T5, 0

        L(BU,64,8), ZERO, 0
        C0011(BU,21,3), SRC+.43, 1   ! offset is BITS: one bit left
        ST(BU,64,8), T6, 0

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this every 64-bit
! field below would straddle two words and be refused.
        SLC, (8)2030.0

ZERO    DD(BU,64,8), 0
ALL1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777
! 3476 = octal 6624.  As seven three-bit bytes, low-order first:
! 4, 2, 6, 6, 0, 0, 0.
SRC     DD(BU,64,8), 3476
! The same seven digits already in eight-bit bytes -- what T1 should
! produce, used here as CM's input so block 4 tests the contraction on
! its own rather than on block 1's output.
WIDE    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000601401004

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T6      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
